    "narrative": "Heading into today\u2019s action, our lineup is anchored by three primary assets with scores exceeding the 70.0 threshold. **Vinnie Pasquantino** (71.91) and **Josh Naylor** (71.88) are our top plays, both capitalizing on a highly favorable matchup against Ryan Johnson. While both face a slight park adjustment, the SP skill differential (+19%) provides a massive ceiling for their counting stats. **James Wood** (71.49) remains our most dynamic threat; batting leadoff against Tong offers elite volume potential, and his xwOBA of 0.404 reinforces his status as a must-start despite the neutral platoon environment. **Corbin Carroll** (70.70) remains essential despite a tougher matchup against Buehler; even with a \"hold\" warning due to the opposing backstop\u2019s arm, his elite sprint speed remains our primary engine for preserving our standing in the stolen base category.\n\n**Brice Turang** (64.24) and **Mickey Moniak** (66.00) provide excellent secondary value, with the former benefiting from a +8% bump for his spot in the #2 hole and the latter exploiting a +14% platoon advantage against Martin. Our most difficult decision centered on **Austin Riley** (53.62), who was the first cut from the active roster. While **Austin Riley** profiles as a reliable veteran, his output against Ralston was projected lower than the rest of our infield core, specifically lagging behind the efficiency of **Gabriel Moreno** and **Isaac Paredes**, who both benefit from superior situational lineup placement. \n\nA few cautionary notes for the evening: **Moises Ballesteros** (43.54) and **Cole Young** (49.12) are currently hovering near our \"Marginal\" floor. Both are awaiting official lineup confirmation, and should either drop down in the batting order, their efficiency scores would likely dip below the 40.0 threshold, necessitating a quick pivot to our bench depth. We are also closely monitoring the potential for high-leverage defensive substitutions late in the game 